WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:16.800
BLAIR: Right. Well first of all, Morgan, I've--

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.259
I mean, when you are the leader of the
country, I think you've got to be the leader of the

00:00:20.259 --> 00:00:21.359
whole country,

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:23.340
so you can't take the narrow view of--

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.890
you can't say, "Look, this-- my--

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:30.169
whatever the policy is, my church overrides,

00:00:30.169 --> 00:00:31.250
you know, the policy

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:34.090
that I think is in the interests of the country. Though

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.020
I think

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.870
the difficult thing here is that-- that--

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:44.110
actually, your religious views always,

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.880
of course,

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.340
color your approach to many

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.710
problems, and probably I would not have taken the
same

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:56.710
attitude to the debt campaign if I
hadn't personally been involved in that church movement.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.260
But that doesn't mean that-- but what you can't
do then is surrender your--

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.750
you know, your-- 

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.590
as it were,

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:08.640
your duty to do what you think is 
right and correct and represent the whole

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:09.729
country to your religion.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:13.369
Now people always think religious people may
do that, but actually, genuinely, they--

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:15.270
usually they don't. In fact,

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.869
when the governor--

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:24.429
I think it was Al Smith who was the first Catholic
ever to stand as a presidential candidate. 

00:01:24.429 --> 00:01:28.759
When he ran for office--

00:01:28.759 --> 00:01:30.959
I think it was the end of the 20s,

00:01:30.959 --> 00:01:31.799
maybe? But

00:01:31.799 --> 00:01:34.610
anyway, there was this

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.020
big-- big campaign that the

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:38.780
Pope was gonna move opposite the White
House, and

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.170
you know, he was therefore--

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:45.670
and I remember, because my wife is a Catholic--
before the 1997 campaign,

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:47.280
the Reverend Ian 

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:51.979
Paisley suggested thatbecause my wife's
allegiance was to Rome, that she couldn't--

00:01:51.979 --> 00:01:53.460
you know, I wouldn't really be

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.540
fit to

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.060
run the country-- and I remember I was so--

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.270
"Rome? What the--

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.750
she got some Italian connection I don't know
about?"

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:05.170
But actually what he meant was-- so, I think
you've got to be careful of that.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:10.499
And the reason-- yeah, the reason,
frankly, I didn't, uh,

00:02:10.499 --> 00:02:14.429
convert to Catholicism when I was Prime Minister
was just-- look, I had enough issues without

00:02:14.429 --> 00:02:16.379
lighting that one into all the others. 

00:02:16.379 --> 00:02:18.719
So, I decided to do it afterwards. But I think,

00:02:18.719 --> 00:02:20.190
you know, as a political leader,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.390
you've got to represent-- in a pluralist

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.139
society, you've got to be able,

00:02:24.139 --> 00:02:25.370
genuinely, to represent

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.049
the whole of the country.

00:02:27.049 --> 00:02:33.349
I think, in relation to what Joe was saying--

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:37.199
I'm not sure about this, actually. I think
there are overlapping principles,

00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:42.769
'cause I think,

00:02:42.769 --> 00:02:46.319
you know, both the lady with the placard and Barney Frank
kind of accept that,

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:47.869
in the end, they're both

00:02:47.869 --> 00:02:51.269
part of the constitution. I think one of the
real strengths of America

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:52.969
is this

00:02:52.969 --> 00:02:58.359
idea that there is indeed a common space that
everyone is-- is in. 

00:02:58.359 --> 00:02:59.679
I mean, one of the things you find--

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:03.559
really interesting out in the Middle East
is when you explain to people

00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:08.959
in certain countries in the Middle East that
as a Muslim, you can worship in America.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:10.299
Because people actually--

00:03:10.299 --> 00:03:13.089
I've had people say to me,

00:03:13.089 --> 00:03:20.089
"No, not America." You say, "No, it's-- millions
of Muslims, you've got mosques in America." "Nah, nah."

00:03:20.409 --> 00:03:25.230
And it's really, you know-- I think-- but that's
because, actually, it's just taken for granted, actually--

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:26.469
this is--

00:03:26.469 --> 00:03:29.149
that in fact these principles do
overlap,

00:03:29.149 --> 00:03:34.199
even with the most extreme people. And I think
that's a very, very important part of a functioning

00:03:34.199 --> 00:03:38.699
society. Very, very important part and not
to be given up lightly. At all. 

00:03:38.699 --> 00:03:39.969
It's something that's

00:03:39.969 --> 00:03:41.639
desperately important. I was explaining

00:03:41.639 --> 00:03:43.110
to people earlier today how

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:46.670
one of the issues I had to tackle when I was Prime
Minister was that there were second generation

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:47.559
immigrants

00:03:47.559 --> 00:03:50.299
who would come into the UK and decide that they--

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:52.140
and still couldn't speak English.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.949
Second generation.

00:03:54.949 --> 00:03:56.329
And my view in the end was

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:57.899
actually-- they--

00:03:57.899 --> 00:04:00.749
it was part of the social obligation to do
this.

00:04:00.749 --> 00:04:04.069
Because otherwise we weren't cohesive
as a society. Now, 

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:07.709
on one level, that's an interference with someone's
freedom. But on another level,

00:04:07.709 --> 00:04:10.099
you need to create that common space.

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:14.419
And you're right, also, that it evolves over
time. Of course it does. This is why any

00:04:14.419 --> 00:04:17.359
religious belief, actually, that is static--

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:18.539
in other words,

00:04:18.539 --> 00:04:21.039
takes no account of its historical setting
is,

00:04:21.039 --> 00:04:24.189
I'm afraid, wrong in the end. And it just-- because

00:04:24.189 --> 00:04:25.609
time evolves. 

00:04:25.609 --> 00:04:28.560
You know, in Christian times, there were slaves. 

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:30.739
St. Paul wrote 

00:04:30.739 --> 00:04:32.619
about slaves having to,

00:04:32.619 --> 00:04:36.300
you know, obviously to-- to accept the authority
of their masters. Now, there's a discourse there

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.380
that in our time time would be completely--

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:41.499
considered completely absurd and unacceptable. 

00:04:41.499 --> 00:04:43.710
But that's the way it is. But I think

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.390
that the value of having

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.289
this concept of the common space

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:49.770
is that it allows that evolution to happen.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.169
And for that space also to be enlarged and changed
over time.

00:04:53.169 --> 00:04:57.779
And Amanda, just finally on your point-- 

00:04:57.779 --> 00:05:02.279
'cause Stephanie, you were asking to-- 

00:05:02.279 --> 00:05:04.919
well, I think

00:05:04.919 --> 00:05:05.929
if the state

00:05:05.929 --> 00:05:08.470
could make that provision-- no, I think

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:11.610
that the religious groups wouldn't have to
fill the gap.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.030
But I think what's 

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.169
fascinating and important

00:05:14.169 --> 00:05:16.199
is that they have filled the gap. 

00:05:16.199 --> 00:05:19.869
And they feel that because people were inspired
by their religious belief.

00:05:19.869 --> 00:05:22.659
And that's why I think that-- that

00:05:22.659 --> 00:05:26.229
this-- as I say, I think

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:29.020
the interesting thing about this whole debate

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:30.509
is that it's

00:05:30.509 --> 00:05:34.960
often put in terms of, "Religious people
must accept that they can't

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.769
decide what happens, as it were, just by 
virtue of their belief."

00:05:39.769 --> 00:05:41.370
But in actual fact I think

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.359
every bit as interesting--

00:05:43.359 --> 00:05:45.199
which is the reason why

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:47.529
Nicholas's response

00:05:47.529 --> 00:05:50.780
to a lot of what was there-- is that

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:53.430
also, people who are non-religious

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:58.619
should be open enough to the religious to
accept that there are certain

00:05:58.619 --> 00:06:03.180
ideas and thoughts and values that actually

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:06.800
can enrich society as a result of that religious
belief, even if you decide,

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.679
"I'm not a person of faith myself."

00:06:11.679 --> 00:06:12.240
WOLTERSTORFF: Let's

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.670
let the other questions get raised. Just one

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:16.349
quick response to what Tony said.

00:06:16.349 --> 00:06:21.119
The the fact that the Muslims in the Middle
East find it incredible that-- when you

00:06:21.119 --> 00:06:22.040
say to them that

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.129
as as a Muslim you can worship in the US--

00:06:24.129 --> 00:06:28.119
that just reinforces

00:06:28.119 --> 00:06:29.939
the analysis I gave, that-- that

00:06:29.939 --> 00:06:32.310
the way we have structured religion in the state

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:36.379
is truly extraordinary, and most people
find it

00:06:36.379 --> 00:06:38.979
difficult to believe. "What do you
mean that

00:06:38.979 --> 00:06:42.259
that all the religions are treated equally? What?"

00:06:42.259 --> 00:06:44.899
Extremely difficult to believe.

00:06:44.899 --> 00:06:49.889
And we forget what a risky experiment it was
250 years ago. Had never been

00:06:49.889 --> 00:06:53.840
tried, and I think what's behind the Habermas
quotation is--

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:56.490
well, I mean, Habermas and

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:00.930
McEntire also think of the state as just like
a telephone company and so forth, a huge bureaucracy--

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:05.569
but it's this nostalgic longing for
once upon a time,

00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:08.540
the state was the highest expression of a

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:12.090
morally, religiously unified citizenry.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:16.229
Now that's gone. And it's just into managements.
I think that's--

00:07:16.229 --> 00:07:19.439
the nostalgia, that's putting it from the

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:23.399
dark frightening side, but James Madison, 
above all, said,

00:07:23.399 --> 00:07:24.240
"It has to be tried."

